{{!
    Google Analytics for every Friends with Habits view.

    These templates are server-rendered and ship no React bundle, so the app's
    normal initialisation path - getGa4Configs() called from a React component in
    src/components - never runs on this host. Until this partial existed that was
    silent: habits.therr.com served real traffic and appeared as a hostname in
    zero GA4 properties, while simultaneously showing up as a referral source in
    another property. Every install driven from the web was unattributed.

    The measurement ids arrive as gaMeasurementIdsJson, a serialize-javascript
    JSON string built by the habits middleware from the same getGa4Configs()
    helper the React clients use, so the two stay on one source of truth. It is a
    triple-stash because it is raw JSON going into a script body, not text going
    into markup.

    When the array is empty the snippet does nothing and no request is made -
    that is the local-dev and misconfiguration case, and analytics must never be
    what breaks a page.

    Two constraints inherited from the sibling footer partial, both of which took
    down every habits page when they were violated there:

      1. No partial call inside this comment. Handlebars executes one even inside
         an HTML comment, and a partial that names itself recurses until the
         stack blows.
      2. No long-form comment delimiter inside this block. The long form ends at
         the FIRST closing delimiter, even one written as prose, which dumps the
         rest of the explanation onto the page as visible copy. Hence the short
         bare-bang form.
}}
<script>
    window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
    function gtag() { window.dataLayer.push(arguments); }
    (function () {
        var ids = {{#if gaMeasurementIdsJson}}{{{gaMeasurementIdsJson}}}{{else}}[]{{/if}};
        if (!Array.isArray(ids) || !ids.length) { return; }
        gtag('js', new Date());
        ids.forEach(function (id) {
            gtag('config', id, {
                surface: 'habits',
                // habits.therr.com and therr.app are different registrable domains.
                // Without the linker a visitor crossing between them starts a fresh
                // session attributed to a referral and the original campaign is lost.
                linker: { domains: ['therr.app', 'therr.com'], accept_incoming: true }
            });
        });
        var tag = document.createElement('script');
        tag.async = true;
        tag.src = 'https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=' + encodeURIComponent(ids[0]);
        document.head.appendChild(tag);
    })();
</script>
{{!
    Attribution capture runs alongside GA4 and on every habits view for the same
    reason: it must see the FIRST page of the session. It is a separate partial
    because the two answer different questions - GA4 reports sessions, this
    writes the record that main."userAcquisition" joins a signup to spend by.
}}
{{> habitsAttribution}}
